Problem:
Suppose  $a$  and  $b$  are real numbers such that  $0 < a < b < 1$ . Let  $$ x= \frac{1}{\sqrt{b}} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{b+a}},\hspace{1cm}
y= \frac{1}{b-a} - \frac{1}{b}\hspace{0.5cm}\textrm{and}\hspace{0.5cm}
z= \frac{1}{\sqrt{b-a}} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{b}}. $$  Show that  $x$ ,  $y$ ,  $z$  are always ordered from smallest to largest in the same way, regardless of the choice of  $a$  and  $b$ . Find this order among  $x$ ,  $y$ ,  $z$ .

Let \[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
ax+3 & \text{ if }x>0, \\
ab & \text{ if }x=0, \\
bx^2+c & \text{ if }x<0.
\end{array}
\right.\]If $f(2)=7$, $f(0)=6$, and $f(-1)=-5$, and $a$, $b$, and $c$ are nonnegative integers, then what is $a+b+c$?
